  and he had to go to court in that state  he went straight from hospital to court  yes because of the torture he had to  undergo three operations he had barely  recovered from the last one when he was  brought back to the court despite the  severe torture Hamid never confessed to  the murder  when we were kids our biggest dream was  to wake up and see a lot of snow on the  ground that would have been the best  thing for us now I get up every day  hoping or dreaming to see a meter of  snow and fatsana and Javid free I looked  straight to my phone first thing in the  morning for news good news  I start my day with that hope  so we're hearing that from the brother  of an Iranian man who was in danger of  being executed speaking to us here at DW  and we've just heard that that death  sentence has now been lifted let's  consider this with the Mahmoud Amiri  mogadam who's an Iranian human rights  Defender and director of the  norway-based non-governmental  organization Iran human rights welcome  to DW  um you must be very pleased to hear this  news  thank you for having me here well uh yes  so this morning several Iranian  newspapers reported about uh lifting of  this or removal of the death sentence  although they say it's possibly removed  um at the order of a higher official and  the higher official normally who has the  authority to do that is  either the supreme leader or head of the  Judiciary himself but the family hasn't  you know received any official notice  yet so we are not sure but if that's the  case of course we are very happy but we  also know that they are a dozen others  uh waiting or at risk of being executed  and and of course  the international attention around the  case makes it more difficult for the  authorities to actually conduct  executions and do you think that that  that obviously was speculating at this  at this point but do you think that is  that might be part of the reason why  this um execution has been lifted the  international attention that this case  has attracted  absolutely because you know the aim of  the executions is to spread fear the  Iranian authorities they have decided to  do some executions of protesters because  they have realized that after three  months of shooting at them on the  streets killing several hundred of them  there is protests continues so they have  escalated this violence by issuing death  sentences and and we know that the death  penalty is the most uh probably powerful  tool to spread fear  um so at the same time they want to pay  as little price as possible for these  executions so cases that are not known  they are in more danger and it's not a  coincidence that the first protester who  was executed most in shikari basically  there were no pictures of him outside we  did not know so much about him  the the thinking behind the the regime  because the world is seeing these uh  protests clearly the regime is trying to  crack down on them but rather than  charging them with uh Riot they the  charges seems vague things like enmity  against God or corruption on Earth why  not charge them say we are putting you  to death because you took part in these  riots and the rest of you pay attention  to this  well you see it  the vague charges of corruption on Earth  or Mojave Waging War against God  it gives the power to the judges to  basically decide and Define who they  want to sentence to that you know we are  not talking about a rule of law or due  process right these revolutionary courts  they are just extension of these  oppressive organs that you see on the  streets so so basically  I was gonna say thank you they just used  it  for talking to uh DW this morning and we  wish you well with your efforts  from Iran human rights
 

